@page "/blogs/stop-nuget-packages-from-corrupting-your-domain-the-anti-corruption-layer-pattern-in-dotnet"
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(NugetAntiCorruptionLayer)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                An <Highlight>Anti-Corruption Layer</Highlight> (ACL) is an application boundary that translates
                between an external library's API and the application's own model. In .NET, an interface and
                adapter can keep package-specific types out of domain-facing code.
            </p>
            <p>
                This article shows how to define an application-owned contract, implement an adapter for a
                third-party package, register it through dependency injection, and make the boundary visible in
                the project structure.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <h4>How Package Types Expand the Change Surface</h4>
            <p>
                Consider a fictional currency library, <Highlight>FxRatesClient</Highlight>, referenced by
                several services. The <Highlight>FxRatesFetcher</Highlight> type and
                <Highlight>FxRate</Highlight> return type from the package appear inside business logic:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// ❌ Dependency leaked — FxRatesClient types appear directly in domain code
using FxRatesClient; // the NuGet package

public class PricingService
{
    private readonly FxRatesFetcher _fetcher; // NuGet type in domain constructor

    public PricingService(FxRatesFetcher fetcher) =&gt; _fetcher = fetcher;

    public decimal ConvertToBaseCurrency(decimal amount, string currencyCode)
    {
        FxRate rate = _fetcher.GetLatestRate(currencyCode); // returns NuGet type
        return amount * (decimal)rate.Multiplier;
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The same pattern appears in <Highlight>QuoteService</Highlight> and
                <Highlight>InvoiceService</Highlight>. <Highlight>FxRate</Highlight>,
                <Highlight>FxRatesFetcher</Highlight>, and <Highlight>FxNetworkException</Highlight> are now
                referenced across eight files in three projects. When <Highlight>FxRatesClient v2</Highlight>
                renames <Highlight>FxRate.Multiplier</Highlight> to <Highlight>FxRate.ConversionFactor</Highlight>
                and restructures its exception hierarchy, every one of those eight files breaks at compile time.
            </p>

            <h4>How Coupling Affects Dependency Updates</h4>
            <p>
                When package types appear across multiple projects, every consumer becomes part of the upgrade
                surface. Tests, helpers, and exception handling may also depend on those types. An application-owned
                contract narrows that surface, although the initial refactoring still requires updating existing
                consumers.
            </p>
            <p>
                Coupling can make a supported update harder to evaluate and deploy. An ACL helps isolate the
                package API, but it does not fix a vulnerable version; teams still need to assess the advisory,
                choose a remediation, and verify the resulting dependency graph.
            </p>

            <h4>An ACL Is an Architectural Decision, Not Just a Refactoring</h4>
            <p>
                Introducing an ACL requires designing a deliberate project boundary — not merely extracting a
                class. That makes it worth recording as an <Highlight>Architectural Decision Record</Highlight>
                (ADR): a short document that captures why the boundary was created, which package it isolates, and
                where the adapter project lives. Without this record, future engineers are likely to bypass the
                boundary, re-introducing the sprawl the ACL was created to prevent.
            </p>
            <p>
                A project boundary is not automatically enforced just because one project declares the direct
                <Highlight>PackageReference</Highlight>. Package assets can flow transitively through project
                references, and a dependency may be added elsewhere later. Use explicit project references and
                add architecture tests, analyzers, or repository checks if you need the build to reject boundary
                violations.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>The Scenario: A Currency Converter Backed by a Third-Party NuGet Package</h4>
            <p>
                Your <Highlight>PricingService</Highlight>, <Highlight>QuoteService</Highlight>, and
                <Highlight>InvoiceService</Highlight>                 all convert amounts to a base currency by directly consuming the fictional
                <Highlight>FxRatesClient</Highlight> NuGet package. The initial refactoring moves these
                consumers to an application-owned contract; later package API changes can remain in the adapter
                when the contract still fits.
            </p>
            <p>
                Define the contract, implement an adapter, update the current consumers, and register the
                adapter in the dependency-injection composition root.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 1 — Define the Abstraction in a Project With Zero NuGet References</h4>
            <p>
                Create an interface in an application abstractions project. Keep its signatures in application
                terms and avoid exposing package-specific types:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Project: Pricing.Application.Abstractions
// .csproj has NO &lt;PackageReference&gt; entries — pure domain contract

namespace Pricing.Application.Abstractions;

public interface ICurrencyConverter
{
    /// &lt;summary&gt;
    /// Converts the specified amount from the given ISO 4217 currency code
    /// to the application's configured base currency.
    /// &lt;/summary&gt;
    decimal ConvertToBase(decimal amount, string isoCurrencyCode);
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Notice that <Highlight>FxRate</Highlight>, <Highlight>FxRatesFetcher</Highlight>, and every
                other type from the package are absent. The interface is written entirely in your application's
                vocabulary — it is part of your domain, not an extension of the external library.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 2 — Create an Isolated Adapter as the Sole Consumer of the NuGet Package</h4>
            <p>
                Create an infrastructure adapter that references <Highlight>FxRatesClient</Highlight>. Keep
                package-specific types in its implementation and translate between the application contract and
                the package API:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Project: Pricing.Infrastructure.Currency
// .csproj: &lt;PackageReference Include="FxRatesClient" Version="1.9.4" /&gt;
// This is the ONLY project allowed to reference FxRatesClient.

using FxRatesClient;
using FxRatesClient.Exceptions;
using Pricing.Application;
using Pricing.Application.Abstractions;

namespace Pricing.Infrastructure.Currency;

public sealed class FxRatesClientAdapter : ICurrencyConverter
{
    private readonly FxRatesFetcher _fetcher;

    public FxRatesClientAdapter(FxRatesFetcher fetcher)
        =&gt; _fetcher = fetcher;

    public decimal ConvertToBase(decimal amount, string isoCurrencyCode)
    {
        try
        {
            FxRate rate = _fetcher.GetLatestRate(isoCurrencyCode);
            return amount * (decimal)rate.Multiplier;
        }
        catch (FxNetworkException ex)
        {
            // Preserve the cause for internal diagnostics; do not return exception details publicly.
            throw new CurrencyConversionException(
                $"Unable to retrieve exchange rate for '{isoCurrencyCode}'.", ex);
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The <Highlight>CurrencyConversionException</Highlight> lives in your domain project and carries
                no reference to <Highlight>FxRatesClient</Highlight>:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Project: Pricing.Application (domain, no NuGet references)

using System;

namespace Pricing.Application;

public sealed class CurrencyConversionException : Exception
{
    public CurrencyConversionException(string message)
        : base(message) { }

    public CurrencyConversionException(string message, Exception innerException)
        : base(message, innerException) { }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Keep provider details in internal logs or exception diagnostics according to your policy.
                Public API responses should expose only the stable application-level error, not raw provider
                messages or stack traces.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 3 — Update Domain Services to Depend on the Abstraction</h4>
            <p>
                Replace direct injections of <Highlight>FxRatesFetcher</Highlight> with
                <Highlight>ICurrencyConverter</Highlight>. This initial migration updates existing consumers:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// ✅ Domain code depends only on the abstraction — no NuGet types here
using Pricing.Application.Abstractions;

public class PricingService
{
    private readonly ICurrencyConverter _currencyConverter;

    public PricingService(ICurrencyConverter currencyConverter)
        =&gt; _currencyConverter = currencyConverter;

    public decimal ConvertToBaseCurrency(decimal amount, string currencyCode)
        =&gt; _currencyConverter.ConvertToBase(amount, currencyCode);
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Apply the same change to <Highlight>QuoteService</Highlight> and
                <Highlight>InvoiceService</Highlight>. The services now depend on the application contract.
                Later changes may still affect the adapter, project references, registration, or tests.
            </p>

            <h4>Step 4 — Register the Adapter via a DI Extension Method in the Infrastructure Project</h4>
            <p>
                To keep package-specific construction in the infrastructure project, add a DI registration
                extension method <em>inside</em> <Highlight>Pricing.Infrastructure.Currency</Highlight>. This
                lets <Highlight>Program.cs</Highlight> call the registration method without directly importing
                <Highlight>FxRatesClient</Highlight>:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Project: Pricing.Infrastructure.Currency
// Same project that already holds FxRatesClientAdapter — no new PackageReference needed.

using FxRatesClient;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Pricing.Application.Abstractions;

namespace Pricing.Infrastructure.Currency;

public static class ServiceCollectionExtensions
{
    public static IServiceCollection AddFxRatesAdapter(
        this IServiceCollection services, string apiKey)
    {
        services.AddScoped(_ =&gt; new FxRatesFetcher(apiKey));
        services.AddScoped&lt;ICurrencyConverter, FxRatesClientAdapter&gt;();
        return services;
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>Program.cs</Highlight> now references only the infrastructure project — it imports
                nothing from <Highlight>FxRatesClient</Highlight> directly:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Program.cs — zero FxRatesClient imports; the boundary holds here too.
using Pricing.Application;
using Pricing.Infrastructure.Currency;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

var apiKey = builder.Configuration["FxRates:ApiKey"]
    ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("FxRates:ApiKey is not configured.");
builder.Services.AddFxRatesAdapter(apiKey);
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;PricingService&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;QuoteService&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;InvoiceService&gt;();
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Project Structure: Make the Boundary Visible</h4>
            <p>
                The structure below makes intended ownership explicit. Add an architecture test or analyzer if
                the repository needs an automated check against leaked package types:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
Solution
├── Pricing.Application.Abstractions   (application contract)
│     └── ICurrencyConverter.cs
│
├── Pricing.Application                (application services)
│     ├── PricingService.cs            → depends on ICurrencyConverter
│     ├── QuoteService.cs              → depends on ICurrencyConverter
│     ├── InvoiceService.cs            → depends on ICurrencyConverter
│     └── CurrencyConversionException.cs
│
├── Pricing.Infrastructure.Currency    (adapter and package reference)
│     ├── FxRatesClientAdapter.cs      → implements ICurrencyConverter
│     └── ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs → AddFxRatesAdapter() DI helper
│
└── Pricing.Api (composition root)
      └── Program.cs                   → calls AddFxRatesAdapter(); no FxRatesClient import
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Keep Future Package Changes Behind the Contract</h4>
            <p>
                With the ACL in place, replacing <Highlight>FxRatesClient</Highlight> — whether with a newer
                package, a different provider, or a no-dependency fallback — can leave domain consumers
                unchanged if the contract remains suitable. This fixed-rate example is suitable only for tests
                or demonstrations, not for current exchange-rate calculations:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Project: Pricing.Application (no PackageReference entries required)

using Pricing.Application;
using Pricing.Application.Abstractions;

namespace Pricing.Application.Currency;

public sealed class FixedRateCurrencyConverter : ICurrencyConverter
{
    // Extend this table from configuration or a database in production
    private static readonly Dictionary&lt;string, decimal&gt; Rates =
        new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
        {
            ["USD"] = 0.79m,
            ["EUR"] = 0.86m,
            ["JPY"] = 0.0053m,
            ["AUD"] = 0.52m,
        };

    public decimal ConvertToBase(decimal amount, string isoCurrencyCode)
    {
        if (!Rates.TryGetValue(isoCurrencyCode, out var rate))
            throw new CurrencyConversionException(
                $"No fixed rate is configured for currency '{isoCurrencyCode}'.");

        return amount * rate;
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                To activate the demonstration fallback, change the registration in
                <Highlight>Program.cs</Highlight>:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Before (uses FxRatesClient via the infrastructure adapter):
// builder.Services.AddFxRatesAdapter(builder.Configuration["FxRates:ApiKey"]!);

// After — add these imports at the top of Program.cs and swap the registration:
// using Pricing.Application.Abstractions;
// using Pricing.Application.Currency;
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;ICurrencyConverter, FixedRateCurrencyConverter&gt;();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>PricingService</Highlight>, <Highlight>QuoteService</Highlight>, and
                <Highlight>InvoiceService</Highlight> do not know — and do not need to know — which
                implementation is active. The abstraction is the contract; the adapter is the interchangeable detail.
            </p>

            <h4>ACL vs. Adapter Pattern: Why the Distinction Matters</h4>
            <p>
                An <Highlight>adapter</Highlight> translates between APIs. An ACL is a broader architectural
                boundary that keeps an external model from shaping application code. Project structure makes the
                boundary visible; architecture tests or analyzers can enforce specific rules. An
                <Highlight>Architectural Decision Record</Highlight> can document why the boundary exists and
                which projects may depend on the package.
            </p>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <p>
                An ACL is useful when package-specific APIs have spread into application code and the cost of
                changing them justifies a boundary. Its purpose is to reduce coupling, not to certify a package
                or resolve an advisory.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Direct use of package types in application code expands the set of consumers affected by
                    package API changes.
                </li>
                <li>
                    An <Highlight>Anti-Corruption Layer</Highlight> defines a seam between application concepts
                    and an external API. Project structure makes the boundary visible; an architecture test,
                    analyzer, or repository rule is needed if it must be enforced automatically.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The four steps are: define a domain interface with no NuGet types, create an isolated
                    infrastructure adapter project as the sole consumer of the package, update domain services to
                    inject the interface, and register the adapter in the DI composition root.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Translate package-specific failures into application-level errors where callers need a stable
                    contract. Keep package exception details in diagnostics only when policy permits.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Replacing a package can leave domain-facing consumers unchanged when the application contract
                    remains stable, though adapter, project, registration, and test changes may still be required.
                </li>
                <li>
                    An ACL is an architectural decision worth recording in an
                    <Highlight>Architectural Decision Record</Highlight> (ADR) so that future engineers understand
                    the boundary and do not inadvertently bypass it.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                To see how an ACL fits into NuGet dependency remediation, read
                <NavLink href="blogs/how-to-find-nuget-dependency-vulnerabilities-before-they-find-you-in-dotnet">How to Find NuGet Dependency Vulnerabilities Before They Find You in .NET</NavLink>
                and
                <NavLink href="blogs/swap-out-a-vulnerable-nuget-package-without-touching-your-domain-code-in-dotnet">Swap Out a Vulnerable NuGet Package Without Touching Your Domain Code in .NET</NavLink>.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
</Content>
